e last element in this list.\r\n  a[4][-1] returns 'hi'.\r\n  * return 'h' in 'hi': a[4][-1][0]\r\n\r\n> for i in a:\r\n    print(i)\r\n    ----------> prints elements of the list.\r\n\r\n  for i in range(len(a)):\r\n    print(i)\r\n    ----------> prints the indexes.\r\n\r\n  for i in range(len(a)):\r\n    print(a[i])\r\n    ----------> prints elements of the list.\r\n\r\n> a.append(<value>) for adding a new element to the END of the list.\r\n> a.insert(<index>, <value>) for adding a new element in position of the given INDEX.\r\n> a.extend(iterable) for extending the list, using an iterable data structure (equal to a-list + b-list).\r\n\r\n* a = [1, 2, 3]:\r\n  a.append([4,5,6])\r\n  -----------------> a = [1, 2, 3, [4, 5, 6]]\r\n  a.extend([4, 5, 6])\r\n  -----------------> a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\r\n\r\n> a.remove(<value>) removes the first item from the list whose value is equal to <value>.\r\n> a.pop(<position>) removes the item at the given position in the list AND returns it.\r\n  * if no index is specified, last item in the list is removed and returned.\r\n> a.clear() clears list.\r\n> a.index(<value>) returns the index of the given value.\r\n> a. count(<value>) returns the number of times, given value appears in the list.\r\n> a.sort() sorts a from lowest to greatest.\r\n> a.reverse() reverses the elements of the list.\r\n> a.copy()\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n
